        <item>
            <title>0.9.0</title>
            <pubDate>Mon, 27 Jul 2026 20:19:16 +0200</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>900</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.9.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.9.0</h2>
<p><b>Speaker Tags — your meeting notes now name who said what.</b> On a Google
Meet call, Jarvis can tag each line of the transcript with the person who actually
said it, instead of a generic "Them" — so notes, action items, and search all know
who's who (e.g. "what did Sam say about the launch?").</p>
<ul>
  <li>A small Jarvis Chrome extension reads the participant names shown on your Meet
      screen and streams them to the app over a local connection — no audio, nothing
      leaves your Mac.</li>
  <li><b>Guided setup.</b> "Set up Speaker Tags" walks you through installing the
      extension: one click stages it and opens Chrome, an on-top panel guides the two
      Chrome steps, and Jarvis confirms the moment it connects — no guessing.</li>
  <li>Meeting notes now show an <b>Attendees</b> list, and far-side speakers appear by
      name in the transcript and the summary.</li>
  <li>Without the extension (or on a solo call) nothing changes — segments stay
      labeled "Them". It's purely additive.</li>
</ul>
<p>Google Meet only for now; Zoom is planned. The transcript for the summary step is
sent to your Anthropic key as before — audio never leaves your Mac.</p>
]]></description>
